  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** means *averse to activity, effort, or movement \u2014 habitually lazy.*\n\nIt describes someone **having or showing a disposition to avoid exertion; slothful.**\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Part of Speech\n**Indolent** is an **adjective** meaning slow or lazy.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c Medical Use\nDoctors also use the word **indolent** to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\nThe word comes from **Late Latin** *indolent-, indolens*, meaning *\"insensitive to pain,\"* from Latin *in-* + *dolent-, dolens*, the present participle of *dol\u0113re*, meaning **\"to feel pain.\"**\n\nThe main modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" dates to around **1710**, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcac Example Sentences\n- *\"The indolent employee spent more time chatting than working.\"*\n- *\"Despite the pressing deadline, he maintained an indolent pace, unconcerned about the consequences.\"*\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd01 Synonyms\nSynonyms of **indolent** include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful.**\n\n---\n\nIn short, **indolent** is a formal and somewhat literary way of calling someone **lazy, idle, or disinclined to work or make effort**.",
